e once i've acquired more words and  have a better ability to comprehend some  people like to start speaking early  whatever people want to do that's fine  but ultimately at some point you need to  speak a lot because you need to practice  that skill if you want to be considered  fluent and we went through the  definition of fluency  then someone also said that they are not  very interested in esperanto because  they didn't like the people or there was  no culture behind it i am also not  interested in esperanto but those people  who are interested in esperanto full  marks to them whatever people are  interested in uh i was expect the same  respect back that if i say i'm not  interested in esperanto the esperantist  should also respect that it's a matter  of pursuing your interests  and then i had a comment here  as  dave here says i don't understand why  you tackle texts way beyond your level  you're just reading complicated text  through the medium of english i don't  know what that means actually i'm  getting used to korean i don't think  there's any real merit in that apart  from it bolstering up bolstering is  enough actually bolstering up  your own ego in that you are reading  something high brow  i don't think political conversations on  this uh  uh  she saw whatever it was called sektanen  chison is highbrow  if you were in korea then surely you'd  have to learn more functional language  and do away with all the political  discussions if anything is very  difficult to steer conversations in that  direction in any language people simply  aren't that interested in having long  debates nor are they particularly  knowledgeable says dave and there's even  a person there who supports him gives  him a one thumb up dave continues steve  is in a privileged position that he will  never have to step outside his comfort  zone never  if he hadn't if he had to move to korea  then he could choose whom he entered who  he interacts with  choose where he wants to live because